\begin_layout Title
Is Positive Or Negative Feedback More Effective?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 1, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Environments with public goods provide a multitude of experimental opportunities
 for researchers interested in the associated theoretical challenges and
 problems.
 Economists, sociologists, and political scientists have all attempted to
 answer a fundamental question concerning the nature of human interaction:
 are people selfish or cooperative? Research on the volutary provision of
 public goods must come to grips with this simple but still unanswered question.
 A reasonable reading of the literature on voluntary contributions will
 lead one to conclude some of the major findings are: face to face communication
 improves the rate of contribution, as well as the threat of possible punishment.
 However, which method is more effective in improving the rate of contribution
 to a public good? Also, if given the choice, would people choose to communicate
, or would they just immediately dole out punishment?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

My experiment mirrors the design of Fehr and Gachter (2000).
 The design consists of a public good experiment six treatment conditions.
 Yet, in this experiment, individuals have the opportunity to communicate
 with one another.
 There is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Stranger
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

-treatment with and without punishment and/or communication opportunities,
 and there is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Partner
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

-treatment with and without punishment and/or communication opportunities.
 Under the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Stranger
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Partner
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 treatments, I will analyze each of the following: (1) cohorts will have
 no opportunity to punish or communicate; (2) cohorts are allowed to communicate
 and punish; (3) cohorts must choose to either punish or communicate, but
 they cannot do both.
 In the Partner-treatment the same group of subjects plays a finitely repeated
 public goods game for several periods, so the group composition does not
 change across periods.
 On the other hand, all of the cohorts in the the Stranger-treatment will
 be randomly partitioned into smaller groups in each of the periods.
 Important to note, while the notion of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Stranger
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

-treatment seems counter-intuitive in the presence of communication opportunitie
s, the treatment itself is just meant to emphasize that cohorts are only
 interacting for one period, as opposed to several periods.
 The treatment with and without punishment and/or communication serves as
 a control for the other two treatments.
 Also, as Fehr and Cachter (2000) point out, the Partner-treatmnet allows
 the opportunity to make within-subject comparisons as well as across-subject
 comparisons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As has been stated previously, the payoffs, parameters and information condition
s closely mirror Fehr and Gachter (2000).
 However, there are still some distinct differences.
 When subjects are able to both communicate and punish, there will be two
 additional stages along with the simultaneous contribution decision in
 each period.
 In this treatment, subjects are able to communicate with one another in
 the first stage for a designated amount of time.
 After communication ends, the typical public goods game is conducted.
 Then, subjects are given the opportunity to simultaneously punish each
 other in the third stage after they are informed about the individual contribut
ions of the other group members.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The treatment in which subjects are able to choose between communication
 is slightly more complicated.
 At first, subjects will be randomly assigned punishment or communication,
 and they will continue this process for a few periods until they are required
 to switch.
 Once all of the subjects have experienced punishment and communication,
 they will then be allowed to make the decision on their own.
 Before the public goods game starts, the subjects will be required to choose
 between punishment and communication.
 Those choosing to communicate will be allowed to send messages to their
 other members of their group prior to the game beginning, even the ones
 who did not choose to communicate.
 However, the individuals who declined to communicate will be unable to
 send responses, they can only receive messages.
 Then, the public goods game proceeds as normal.
 Finally, the people who chose punishment will be allowed to punish the
 other other members of their group, and this process will proceed as described
 before
\end_layout

